      "mikuna chocho protein powder",
      "mikuna chocho plant protein",
      "is mikuna third party tested",
      "chocho protein powder review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant-Based Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Mikuna Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone looking at Mikuna's Chocho Plant Protein: what the Andean lupin bean actually gives you per scoop, what the published lab reports show, what it costs per 25 g of protein, and the cheaper plant proteins to buy if price matters more than the bean.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#what-is-mikuna">What Is Mikuna</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#allergy">Lupin Allergy</a>
      <a href="#vs">Mikuna vs Pea Protein</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Mikuna protein powder is a single-ingredient Andean lupin (chocho) powder that gives 20 g of protein and 8 g of fiber in a 39 g scoop, per the label at the time of writing, and Mikuna publishes its Eurofins test reports. The catch is price: at about $3.67 per 25 g it costs four to five times what a pea isolate does. Below: label, testing, cost and alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Mikuna Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mikuna is a genuinely different plant protein with unusually open testing and a steep price. Every current bag gives 20 g of protein per serving from chocho, an Andean lupin bean milled whole, per the labels at the time of writing. Mikuna publishes Eurofins reports showing lead below 0.010 mg/kg, no detected PFAS and no detected pesticides in Pure Chocho. It costs about $3.67 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw, and it carries 8 to 9 g of fat per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a one-ingredient, whole-food protein with real fiber, you cook or bake with protein powder, you follow a low FODMAP diet (Pure Chocho is certified), or published lab results matter more to you than price. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are allergic to peanuts or other legumes without a doctor's go-ahead, you are drug tested in sport, or you want the most protein per dollar or per calorie. A pea isolate gives more protein for a fifth of the cost.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Question</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Answer (per label or brand, at the time of writing)</th>
