                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-mikuna" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is Mikuna Chocho Protein Powder, and Who Makes It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mikuna Chocho Plant Protein is a powder made by milling the whole chocho bean, an Andean lupin (<em>Lupinus mutabilis</em>) grown at around 11,000 feet in the Ecuadorian highlands, according to Mikuna. The brand says the beans come from Indigenous Kichwa farming communities and that the whole bean goes into the bag, which is why the powder keeps its fiber and fat instead of being stripped down to an isolate the way most pea and soy proteins are.
          </p>

          <p>
            That single fact explains most of what follows. A protein isolate is the result of washing away carbohydrates, fiber and fat until 80 to 90 percent of what is left is protein. Mikuna does not do that. Its own lot certificate, published on its testing page, measured 54.2 g of protein per 100 g of Pure Chocho, about 21 g in a 39 g scoop. That is a high figure for a whole legume flour, but it means roughly half of every scoop is something other than protein: fiber, fat and minerals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw lupins are bitter because of natural alkaloids, so they have to be debittered before eating. Mikuna says it uses a proprietary debittering process and that the finished powder is free of lectins, and its testing page links a January 2025 report from Certified Group, an ISO/IEC 17025 accredited lab, recording lectins as absent by SDS-PAGE. We have not seen independent research on Mikuna's specific process, so treat the lectin claim as the brand's, backed by that single report.
          </p>

          <p>
            The line is small. Mikuna sells Pure Chocho (one ingredient), Vanilla and Cacao (five ingredients each) and Cinnamon Horchata (seven ingredients), in 15-serving bags and boxes of eight travel sachets. When we checked mikunafoods.com on 21 November 2026, the 15-serving Cinnamon Horchata bag and the 30-serving Pure Chocho bag were listed as sold out, while Horchata sachets were in stock. Mikuna also sells through Erewhon markets in Los Angeles and ships internationally through iHerb, per the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Mikuna Chocho Protein and the Cheaper Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mikuna sells its Chocho Plant Protein on mikunafoods.com and says it is also on Amazon. When we searched, the 15-serving bags were listed by third-party sellers (Captivating Bargains LLC and FHS Retail) rather than by Mikuna or Amazon itself, so check the seller and the best-by date when you order. The first two cards are Mikuna bags at the Amazon price we saw on 21 November 2026. The last two are single-source pea proteins for readers who want the most protein per dollar. Cost per 25 g uses each label's servings and protein.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Mikuna Chocho Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Mikuna flavor gives 20 g of protein per serving, with 190 to 210 calories and 8 to 9 g of fiber, per the nutrition panels printed on the bags at the time of writing. The difference between flavors is small: the sweetened versions use a slightly bigger 43 g scoop, add 115 mg of sodium from sea salt, and carry 1 to 2 g of added sugar from organic coconut blossom nectar.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
