          <p>
            The fat is the number most buyers miss. Pure Chocho carries 8 g of fat per scoop, mostly unsaturated (4 g monounsaturated and 2.5 g polyunsaturated, per the panel). That is not a flaw, since it is the bean's own fat, but it is why a 20 g protein serving costs 190 calories when a pea isolate delivers 24 to 27 g of protein for about 120. Protein makes up about 42 percent of Pure Chocho's calories, against roughly 80 to 90 percent for an isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fiber is the upside. Eight grams is 29 percent of the daily value, per the label, and all of Pure Chocho's 8 g of carbohydrate is fiber, which is why Mikuna markets it as zero net carb and keto-friendly. Most people fall short on fiber, so a scoop that supplies a quarter of it alongside 20 g of protein is useful, but it also means a large serving can feel filling or cause gas while your gut adjusts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is Mikuna a complete protein?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. The bag prints a typical amino acid profile showing all nine essential amino acids, with 1,361 mg of leucine, 893 mg of isoleucine and 768 mg of valine per serving, about 3.0 g of BCAAs in total. Lysine is solid at 1,147 mg. The weak spot, as with most legumes, is the sulfur amino acids: methionine plus cystine comes to 394 mg. The leucine figure is below the 2 to 3 g per meal often cited for muscle building, so lifters may want a bigger serving or another protein in the same meal.
          </p>
