        <div id="buyer-feedback" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Do Buyers Say About Mikuna's Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly describe Pure Chocho as mild and nutty, and the sweetened flavors as lightly sweet rather than dessert-like. We have not tasted it; this section summarizes the reviews posted on mikunafoods.com at the time of writing, which skew positive because they sit on the brand's own store. Mikuna describes Pure Chocho as neutral and nutty, and its FAQ says the most popular flavor shifts between Cacao, Vanilla and Pure Chocho.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common praise is about digestion rather than flavor. Several reviewers say Mikuna is the only protein powder that does not bother their stomach, and some mention that they cannot tolerate whey or pea protein. One reviewer on a low FODMAP diet says it works for them, which fits Pure Chocho's Monash certification. Others like the short ingredient list and use it in oats, juice, muffins and smoothies rather than as a plain shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            On flavor, Cacao and Vanilla come up most often as favorites, and Cinnamon Horchata has a loyal following. The most repeated complaint we saw is availability: multiple reviewers ask for Cinnamon Horchata to come back after the bag version disappeared, and it was still sold out in bag form when we checked. If horchata is the flavor you want, our <a href="/best-horchata-protein-powder/">horchata protein powder guide</a> lists the alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing is the other caveat. Because Mikuna is a whole-bean flour rather than an isolate, it does not dissolve the way whey or pea isolate does. One reviewer notes it does not fully dissolve in warm water, and Mikuna itself suggests stirring a 39 g serving into 16 oz of liquid or blending it. Expect some body and texture, which many people like in smoothies and which works well in baking, but which can feel gritty in a shaker bottle with plain water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mikuna's own suggestions lean into that texture: replace 25 to 33 percent of the flour in pancakes, breads, muffins or arepas, stir one to two tablespoons into oats or yogurt, or add a tablespoon to coffee. Used that way, the price per day drops and the mixing complaint mostly goes away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="allergy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Mikuna Safe If You Have a Peanut or Legume Allergy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not without checking with your doctor first. Chocho is a lupin, and lupin is a legume allergen; Mikuna's label and FAQ both say that people with allergies to lupin, peanuts, peas, soybeans or other legumes should consult a medical professional before trying it. Lupin can cross-react with peanut in people with peanut allergy, which is why it is one of the 14 allergens that must be declared on food labels in the European Union.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mikuna also prints a shared-facility statement: the powder is produced in a facility that handles eggs, wheat, milk, tree nuts, peanuts, soybeans, shellfish and fish. Its December 2025 test found gluten under 3 ppm and no soy DNA in the sample, but the brand itself notes that cross-contact is possible. If you have a severe allergy to any of those foods, the shared facility matters as much as the ingredient list.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, Mikuna's allergen profile is a selling point. It contains no dairy, soy, gluten ingredients, eggs or nuts, which makes it a reasonable option for people avoiding the most common allergens in protein powders. It also skips the gums, sugar alcohols and sucralose that some people blame for bloating, according to the ingredient lists.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is Mikuna low FODMAP?</h3>

          <p>
            Pure Chocho is Monash University Low FODMAP Certified at a standard serving, per Mikuna; the Vanilla, Cacao and Cinnamon Horchata versions are not covered. That is unusual for a legume, since most beans are restricted early in a low FODMAP diet. If you are following one, stick to Pure Chocho and see our <a href="/best-low-fodmap-protein-powder/">low FODMAP protein powder guide</a> for other certified options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual protein powder caveats apply too. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take medication, or manage a medical condition, ask your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. And because each scoop has 8 g of fiber, start with a half serving if you are not used to high-fiber foods.
          </p>
        </div>

