        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Mikuna, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mikuna is worth buying for people who value a whole-food, one-ingredient protein with fiber and open lab testing, and who will pay a premium for it. It is a poor fit for anyone shopping on cost per gram, anyone drug tested in sport, and anyone with a peanut or legume allergy who has not cleared it with a doctor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Mikuna if you</h3>

          <p>
            cook and bake with protein powder and want an ingredient that behaves like a flour; follow a low FODMAP diet and want a certified option (Pure Chocho only); react badly to whey, pea or the gums and sweeteners in mainstream shakes; want a protein with 8 g of fiber and 0 mg of sodium; or care about reading the actual lab numbers for lead, PFAS and pesticides before you buy. Buyers with sensitive digestion are the group most enthusiastic about it in the reviews we read.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip Mikuna if you</h3>

          <p>
            need the cheapest protein per gram, since a pea isolate costs a fifth as much; want 25 to 30 g of protein in a low-calorie scoop for a cut; compete in a tested sport and need NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport; have a lupin, peanut or other legume allergy; or want a shake that dissolves smoothly in water. In those cases the Naked Pea and NOW Sports Pea cards above, or the picks in our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder guide</a>, are better matches.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do try it, start with a 15-serving bag of Pure Chocho rather than a subscription, since it is the version with the published lab reports and the low FODMAP certification. Use it in smoothies and baking first, where its texture helps, and buy direct when you can reach the $70 free-shipping threshold or from a reputable Amazon seller with a clear best-by date.
          </p>
