          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Mikuna to Isolates by the Scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 39 g scoop of Pure Chocho gives 20 g of protein because it is a whole-bean powder, while a 30 g scoop of pea isolate gives 27 g. Judge Mikuna on what it is, a protein-rich flour with fiber and fat, and compare cost per 25 g of protein rather than price per bag or per scoop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Lupin Allergy Warning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chocho is a lupin, a legume that can cross-react with peanut. Mikuna's label tells anyone allergic to lupin, peanuts, peas, soy or other legumes to consult a doctor first. Do not treat it as hypoallergenic just because it is dairy-free, soy-free and gluten-free; for some buyers it is a new allergen.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Every Flavor Is Low FODMAP</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Only Pure Chocho carries Mikuna's Monash University Low FODMAP certification. Vanilla, Cacao and Cinnamon Horchata add coconut blossom nectar, monk fruit and other ingredients and are not covered. If you are following a low FODMAP plan for IBS, buy the unflavored bag and add your own low FODMAP flavorings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Lab Reports as a Sport Certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mikuna's Eurofins reports cover heavy metals, pesticides, PFAS and gluten, which is useful for everyday buyers. They do not screen for banned performance-enhancing substances. Athletes in tested sport need NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport on the exact product, and Mikuna holds neither, per our search.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking It in Plain Water and Giving Up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A whole-bean powder does not dissolve like an isolate, and buyers note it stays slightly gritty in water. Blend it into a smoothie, stir it into oats or swap it for a quarter of the flour in pancakes. Mikuna works best as a cooking ingredient, which is also how it gets cheaper per day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Mikuna third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Mikuna publishes Eurofins laboratory reports for Pure Chocho covering lead, cadmium, mercury, arsenic, pesticides, glyphosate, 16 PFAS compounds and gluten, with the December 2025 results showing lead below 0.010 mg/kg and no PFAS or pesticides detected. It says the panel runs twice a year. Mikuna is not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, so it is not screened for banned substances.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Mikuna protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  By label, it is one of the cleanest protein powders: Pure Chocho lists a single ingredient, chocho powder, with 20 g of protein, 8 g of fiber, 0 g of sugar and 0 mg of sodium per serving. Flavored bags add only coconut nectar, monk fruit, sea salt and vanilla or cacao. It does carry 8 to 9 g of fat, so it is higher in calories than an isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Mikuna contain lead or heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Mikuna's December 2025 Eurofins report on Pure Chocho found lead, cadmium and mercury each below 0.010 mg/kg, or under 0.39 mcg of lead per 39 g scoop, and total arsenic at 0.045 mg/kg. Those are trace levels below Prop 65's 0.5 mcg daily lead benchmark. The product page still carries a Prop 65 lead warning, and flavored versions are not separately reported.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Mikuna flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyers most often name Cacao and Vanilla as favorites in the reviews on Mikuna's site, and Cinnamon Horchata has devoted fans who want it back in bag form. Pure Chocho is the pick for cooking, baking and savory recipes because it is unsweetened and nutty. It is also the only flavor covered by the low FODMAP certification and the published lab reports.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the most common Mikuna complaints?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The complaints we saw are about price, availability and texture rather than quality. At about $3.67 per 25 g of protein it costs far more than pea protein. Reviewers repeatedly ask for Cinnamon Horchata to return, and some note the powder does not fully dissolve in water. Praise centers on easy digestion and the short ingredient list.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Mikuna contain soy, dairy or gluten?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Mikuna's ingredients contain no soy, dairy or gluten, and its December 2025 test found gluten under 3 ppm and no soy DNA in a Pure Chocho sample. However, the label says it is produced in a facility that also handles eggs, wheat, milk, tree nuts, peanuts, soybeans, shellfish and fish, so people with severe allergies should weigh cross-contact risk.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Mikuna keto and low FODMAP?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Pure Chocho has 8 g of carbohydrate, all of it fiber, per the label, so it has essentially zero net carbs and suits keto; flavored bags add 1 to 2 g of sugar. Pure Chocho is Monash University Low FODMAP Certified, per Mikuna, but the Vanilla, Cacao and Horchata versions are not. Check with a dietitian if you follow a medical diet.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
